                "To understand how this wipeout unfolds, consider a founding team that issues three million dollars in post-money SAFEs across various tranches at an effective weighted valuation cap of ten million dollars, granting seed investors thirty percent of the company. The founders assume they retain seventy percent. However, eighteen months later, when negotiating their Series A term sheet for a ten-million-dollar investment at a thirty-million-dollar pre-money valuation, the lead venture capital firm insists on two standard conditions: first, that an unallocated employee option pool of fifteen percent be established on a post-financing basis; and second, that all option pool expansion must occur inside the pre-money valuation.",
                "Now examine the mathematical disaster that ensues. In the pre-money valuation calculation, the incoming venture firm mandates that the thirty percent post-money SAFE dilution AND the fifteen percent post-financing option pool must be created entirely out of the existing capitalization prior to new money investment. Because post-money SAFEs are legally insulated from dilution caused by other seed securities, the entire burden of the option pool creation falls exclusively on the common founders. When the final post-Series A cap table is calculated, the founders discover that the Series A lead owns twenty-five percent, the option pool holds fifteen percent, and the SAFE investors hold thirty percent.",
